In this study, I have explored the development of my gender identity through the combined perspectives of scientific naturalism, materialism, and analytic autoethnography. By bringing personal memories and cross-validated material records into dialogue with the predictive processing framework and the Thinking Through Other Minds model, I have traced how external cultural categories can become embodied as pre-reflective components of a generative self-model. The formation of gender identity does not require an immutable prenatal brain essence or an immaterial gendered soul. Instead, persistent prediction errors, generated by interactions with gendered social expectations, uniform regulations, names, and communicative conflicts, prompt the brain to update its self-model to maintain coherence within its cultural niche.

This analysis shows that physicalism and social constructivism are conceptually complementary. When cultural conventions assign gendered significance to bodily traits, clothing, or personality characteristics, collective intentionality establishes social facts @searle1995construction. Through immersion in cultural practice, an individual internalises these social facts as predictive priors. These priors operate swiftly and automatically below the level of conscious deliberation, generating a subjective feeling of innateness even when the underlying traits are historically and socially learned. A naturalistic constructivist framework thus provides an intelligible alternative for transgender individuals who find neither metaphysical essentialism nor post-structuralist anti-realism adequate for understanding their lived experience.

This framework also clarifies the ethical and political standing of acquired identities. The widespread view that an identity warrants moral respect only if it can be proven biologically innate rests on a naturalistic fallacy. Cultural acquisition does not make an identity superficial, nor does it imply that an identity is a trivial preference that can be discarded at will. Because predictive priors become embedded deep within hierarchical cognitive models and form central components of narrative identity, they resist external coercion and cannot be renounced by voluntary choice. More fundamentally, the ethical obligation to respect an individual's gender identity does not depend on its developmental history. Rooted in Kantian practical reason, moral respect is owed to persons as autonomous rational agents with the capacity to author their own lives @Kant_2011. Coercive attempts to alter an individual's identity violate this autonomy by treating persons as instruments of external social norms.

Subjecting personal experience to the analytical standards of cognitive science and evolutionary biology does not diminish its authenticity. An experience can be acquired through social learning, deeply embodied in neural wetware, and fully worthy of care and dignity, while the historical conditions that produced it remain open to empirical and philosophical critique. This perspective allows us to respect the individual in their present embodied reality while sustaining a rational, naturalistic critique of the cultural structures that shape human self-understanding.